// Practice quiz: Deduplicating: in SQL vs in Python
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. First draft, not reviewed. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/data-pipelines/deduplicating-in-sql-vs-in-python

::o1 unhashable::What does set(rows) do when rows is a list of dicts? {
=Raises TypeError, because dicts are unhashable #Correct. Set members must be hashable; a dict is not.
~Removes duplicate rows #Not quite. It fails before that, and it would need a key to compare.
~Keeps the last row per key #Not quite. A set has no idea what your key is.
~Returns an empty set #Not quite. It raises an error.
}

::o1 first vs last::A loop stores by_id[r["id"]] \= r for every row. Which row is kept per id? {
~The first to arrive #Not quite. That needs a check for whether the key was already seen.
~The one with the newest timestamp #Not quite. Nothing here compares timestamps.
=The last to arrive #Correct. Each assignment replaces the earlier value for that key.
~An arbitrary one #Not quite. It is defined\: the last assignment wins.
}

::o2 distinct on::In PostgreSQL, what must be true of a query using DISTINCT ON (k) with an ORDER BY? {
~The ORDER BY must not mention k #Not quite. It must start with k.
~DISTINCT ON cannot be used with ORDER BY #Not quite. ORDER BY is what decides which row is kept.
=The ORDER BY must start with k #Correct. The DISTINCT ON expressions must match the leftmost ORDER BY expressions.
~k must be a primary key #Not quite. It can be any expression; its values are what repeat.
}

::o2 window filter::Why does WHERE ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...) \= 1 in the same SELECT fail? {
~ROW_NUMBER needs an argument #Not quite. It takes none.
=Window functions run after WHERE, so filter in an outer query #Correct. Wrap the ranking in a subquery or CTE, then filter.
~Only DISTINCT ON can pick one row per key #Not quite. ROW_NUMBER does it too, with the outer filter.
~The number starts at 0 #Not quite. It starts at 1; the placement is the issue.
}

::o3 cross batch::A record can reappear in next month's file. Why is a Python set in today's run not enough? {
~Sets cannot hold strings #Not quite. They can.
~Sets lose items between iterations #Not quite. A set keeps its items for the whole run.
~Python cannot read monthly files #Not quite. It can; memory of earlier runs is the gap.
=It only knows about this run; earlier data is in the database #Correct. Cross-batch duplicates need a check against stored data, such as an upsert or SQL deduplication.
}
